{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And you shall not set up a pillar for yourself</b> — a single stone pillar to offer upon, even to Heaven. <b>Which He hated</b>: He commanded to make an altar of earth and an altar of stones, but this He hated, for it had become a statute for the Canaanites. And although it was beloved to Him in the days of the Patriarchs, now He hated it, since these made it a statute for idolatry — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 16:22). If so, the verse forbade the Asherah, and forbade all planting on the Temple Mount even though one's intention is for Heaven, and forbade the pillar even for Heaven — all three because of the statutes of the Canaanites. But I have not understood this statute, for the Canaanites practiced with altars as well as with pillars, as the verse says, <i>and you shall tear down their altars and smash their pillars</i> (Deuteronomy 12:3), <i>for their altars you shall tear down and their pillars you shall smash</i> (Exodus 34:13), and so in every place. Perhaps one could say that the Canaanites were especially attached to pillars, and they had no house of idolatry without a pillar there to burn incense upon and to pour oil on its head, in the manner stated regarding the Baal (II Kings 10:26), <i>and they brought out the pillars of the house of the Baal</i>; but the altars — in some of them they would also offer. And what seems right in my eyes is that the Canaanites, who were steeped in idolatry, would make in all their houses of gods an altar to offer sacrifices upon it, and a large stone set up at the entrance of the house for the priests to stand upon, and a tree planted outside it to adorn the way of those coming there — and the Christians do likewise to this day. Now the glorious Name hated and rejected all their practices, and forbade the pillar and the Asherah, and left only the altar, which is a requirement of the sacrifices that He, blessed be He, commanded in them, and which were a satisfaction before Him, for He said <i>and let His will be done</i> from before idolatry existed in the world:" "And the reason for <b>which He hated</b> is an explanation that HaShem commanded (Exodus 20:24–25) <i>an altar of earth you shall make for Me</i>, and <i>if an altar of stones you shall make for Me</i>, and Moses explained that in these He takes pleasure, to exclude the pillar, for He hated it, for all their practices are hateful before Him, and He commanded Israel not to do as their practices. But in the days of Jacob it was not yet said, <i>and in their statutes you shall not walk</i> (Leviticus 18:3), and therefore he would use a pillar for the sake of Heaven, according to the custom of the worshippers. Now this is a commandment explained from <i>and if an altar of stones you shall make for Me</i>, but what the Torah says (Leviticus 26:1), <i>and a carved image and a pillar you shall not set up for yourselves</i> — that is a carved image and a pillar to seat the idol upon the pillar for idolatry, for there the verse speaks in the context of idolatry. But He says (ibid.), <i>and a stone pavement you shall not place in your land to prostrate yourselves upon it</i> — even for Heaven — because it appears as though one worships those celestial beings, like the reason for the Asherah, which is from the statutes of the peoples. And know that an altar is a structure of stones, high, and it has four corners, and it has a surrounding base; one ascends by its base and offers between its corners. And the pillar is a single large stone; they would raise it up to burn incense upon it or to offer upon it, or for the priests to stand upon it. And the high place (במה) is like a mountain, and they make it of gathered earth, and upon it a built altar to offer in it; for everything high and elevated is called by that name — <i>I will ascend the heights of the dense cloud</i> (Isaiah 14:14), <i>upon the heights of the earth</i> (Deuteronomy 32:13); even the back of a person is called a במה — <i>upon their heights you shall tread</i> (Deuteronomy 33:29). And so the verse says (I Kings 3:4), <i>and the king went to Gibeon to slaughter there, for it was the great high place; a thousand burnt offerings Solomon would offer upon that altar</i>:"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The closing phrase 'ונעשה רצונו מאז קודם היות עז\"ז' is rendered hyper-literally; it appears to invoke the idea that sacrificial worship performed God's prior will before idolatry arose, but the syntax is compressed and the referent of the quoted or allusive phrase is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:02.534011725Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}